## Abstract

Practical quantum computing will require error rates that are well below what is achievable with physical qubits. Quantum error correction offers a path to algorithmically-relevant error rates by encoding logical qubits within many physical qubits, where increasing the number of physical qubits enhances protection against physical errors. However, introducing more qubits also increases the number of error sources, so the density of errors must be sufficiently low in order for logical performance to improve with increasing code size. Here, we report the measurement of logical qubit performance scaling across multiple code sizes, and demonstrate that our system of superconducting qubits has sufficient performance to overcome the additional errors from increasing qubit number. We find our distance-5 surface code logical qubit modestly outperforms an ensemble of distance-3 logical qubits on average, both in terms of logical error probability over 25 cycles and logical error per cycle ($2.914\% \pm 0.016\%$ compared to $3.028\% \pm 0.023\%$). To investigate damaging, low-probability error sources, we run a distance-25 repetition code and observe a $1.7\times10^{-6}$ logical error per round floor set by a single high-energy event ($1.6\times10^{-7}$ when excluding this event).

## 1 Introduction

Since Feynman's proposal, applications include factoring, optimization, quantum simulation. State-of-the-art physical error rates $\sim 10^{-3}$ per gate — insufficient for large circuits. Quantum error correction (QEC) can exponentially suppress logical errors in proportion to code distance:

$$ \varepsilon_L \propto \left(\tfrac{p}{p_{\text{th}}}\right)^{\lfloor d/2 \rfloor + 1} \qquad (p < p_{\text{th}}) $$

## 2 Surface codes with superconducting qubits

The rotated surface code encodes one logical qubit in $d \times d$ data qubits with $d^2 - 1$ measure (ancilla) qubits interleaved. Logical operators $Z_L$ (top-to-bottom) and $X_L$ (left-to-right) traverse the array. Stabilizer plaquettes (X and Z) are measured every cycle; a syndrome-history is decoded (MWPM here) to infer the physical-error configuration.

## 3 Logical performance (headline result)

Comparing the $d=5$ code to the average of four $d=3$ quadrant subgrids, both run for **25 cycles**:

|                       | $d=3$ (avg of 4 subgrids)   | $d=5$                        |
|-----------------------|-----------------------------|------------------------------|
| Logical error / cycle | $\varepsilon_3 = (3.028 \pm 0.023)\%$ | $\varepsilon_5 = (2.914 \pm 0.016)\%$ |
| Suppression $\Lambda_{3/5} = \varepsilon_3 / \varepsilon_5$ | $\approx 1.04$ (>1, modest) | |

Thus the $d=5$ logical qubit **outperforms the ensemble average** of the $d=3$ subgrids — the first demonstration in a real device that QEC begins to improve performance with code size.

## 4 Fig 4c: threshold behavior under simulated noise scale $s$

- $s=1.3$: above threshold — larger $d$ is worse ($\Lambda<1$).
- $s\in[1.0, 1.2]$: near threshold — turnaround at intermediate $d$.
- $s=0.9$: below threshold — monotone suppression ($\Lambda>1$).

## 5 Distance-25 repetition-code floor

Running a $d=25$ repetition code, the observed logical-error floor is:
- $1.7 \times 10^{-6}$ per round (with a single high-energy event included)
- $1.6 \times 10^{-7}$ per round (excluding that event)

## 6 What this paper does and does not resolve

- Achieves $\Lambda_{3/5} > 1$ but only marginally; text: _"our device is close to threshold; reaching algorithmically-relevant logical error rates … requires $\Lambda \gg 1$"_.
- Extrapolates that per-gate error rates must improve by at least 20% to move well below threshold.

